        <!-- SECTION 2: Water Resistant vs Waterproof: What the Words Actually Mean -->
        <div id="waterproof" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Water Resistant vs Waterproof: What the Words Actually Mean
          </h2>

          <p>
            Laminate marketing uses both terms and they describe genuinely different things. Understanding the distinction prevents the most expensive mistake in the category.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Core Is the Vulnerability</h3>

          <p>
            Laminate is built on a high-density fibreboard core, which is a wood product. When water reaches it, it swells, and unlike a dent that difference is permanent — the plank does not shrink back when it dries. The surface layer is already impervious; water gets in at the seams and edges.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Water Resistant</h3>

          <p>
            Means the surface and usually the joints are treated to keep spills out for a stated period, commonly 24 to 72 hours. Wipe up a spilled glass within that window and nothing happens. It is a real and useful property, and it covers the overwhelming majority of household accidents.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Waterproof</h3>

          <p>
            In laminate this generally still refers to surface and joint protection rather than a core that is immune to water. A genuinely waterproof floor with no wood content is vinyl, where the core is plastic. Read what the warranty actually covers rather than the word on the box.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where Neither Is Enough</h3>

          <p>
            A dishwasher that leaks unnoticed under a cabinet, a burst supply line, or a basement slab that wicks moisture from below will all defeat any laminate. If the room has a real flood risk or persistent subfloor moisture, vinyl or tile is the honest recommendation.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Vapour Barriers Matter on Concrete</h3>

          <p>
            Concrete slabs release moisture continuously, and installing laminate over one without a vapour barrier is how floors cup and swell from underneath with no visible spill. Six mil polyethylene, or an underlayment with a barrier built in, is not optional over a slab.
          </p>
        </div>

