        <!-- SECTION 4: Thickness, Sound, and Why Cheap Laminate Sounds Fake -->
        <div id="thickness" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Thickness, Sound, and Why Cheap Laminate Sounds Fake
          </h2>

          <p>
            Thickness barely affects durability, which is governed by the wear layer and its AC rating. What it changes is how the floor feels and sounds, and that is what makes people believe laminate looks cheap even when it looks convincing.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Thin planks flex and click.</strong> At 6 to 8mm over a hard subfloor, each footstep produces a hollow report because there is an air gap beneath a thin, stiff board. That sound is the single most recognisable tell of budget laminate, and no amount of visual quality overrides it.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Thicker planks sound solid.</strong> At 10 to 12mm the plank has enough mass to damp its own vibration, and the floor reads underfoot much closer to engineered wood. In our walking tests the difference between 8mm and 12mm on identical subfloor was immediately audible to everyone who tried it.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Underlayment does much of the work.</strong> A quality acoustic pad between subfloor and plank absorbs impact sound and is the cheapest available improvement to how a floor feels. Many laminates ship with foam already attached, which is convenient and removes the option of using something better.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Thicker planks bridge subfloor flaws.</strong> A 12mm board spans a small dip without flexing into it, where an 8mm board follows the dip and eventually develops a soft spot or a failing joint. On an older subfloor, thickness buys tolerance.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Height matters at the doorway.</strong> Thick plank plus underlayment can raise a floor enough that doors bind and transitions to adjoining rooms become awkward. Undercutting jambs is straightforward; discovering the problem after the floor is down is not.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">Do Not Double Up Underlayment</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              If your laminate has foam already attached, adding a separate underlayment beneath it is a mistake, not an upgrade. The combined layer is too soft, the planks flex more than the locking system tolerates, and joints eventually separate or crack. Use one or the other. The exception is a vapour barrier over concrete, which is a thin film rather than a cushion and belongs under everything.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 5: Installing It Yourself: What Actually Goes Wrong -->
        <div id="installation" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Installing It Yourself: What Actually Goes Wrong
          </h2>

          <p>
            Laminate is genuinely one of the most DIY-friendly floors available, and the failures are consistent enough to list. None of them are about skill with tools.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Skipping acclimation.</strong> Laminate expands and contracts with humidity. Boxes brought straight from a cold warehouse into a heated house and laid immediately will move after installation, buckling in the middle or gapping at the edges. Leave the boxes flat in the room for at least 48 hours first.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Omitting the expansion gap.</strong> A floating floor must be free to move, which means a gap of roughly 3/16 of an inch at every wall, pipe, and fixed obstacle, hidden later by skirting or trim. Laminate butted tight to a wall has nowhere to go when it expands and will lift into a ridge across the room.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Not checking the subfloor for flatness.</strong> Most manufacturers specify flatness within about 3/16 of an inch over 10 feet. Beyond that, planks flex over the dips, joints work loose, and the floor develops soft, creaking areas. Checking with a long straightedge before you start is far easier than fixing it afterwards.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Fastening the floor down.</strong> A floating floor is not attached to anything, including through transitions, cabinet toe kicks, or heavy fixed furniture. Anything that pins the field prevents movement and causes buckling. Transitions must allow the floor to slide beneath them.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Ignoring plank layout.</strong> Staggering end joints at least 12 inches apart, and avoiding a repeating stair pattern, is what makes a floor read as natural rather than assembled. It costs nothing but attention, and it is obvious in the finished result.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 6: Laminate vs Luxury Vinyl Plank -->
        <div id="vs-vinyl" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Laminate vs Luxury Vinyl Plank
          </h2>

          <p>
            These two categories compete directly and the honest answer is that they are better at different things rather than one being superior.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where Laminate Wins</h3>

          <p>
            Visual and textural realism. Because the surface is a printed layer beneath a hard melamine wear coat with deep registered embossing, high-end laminate reads as wood more convincingly than most vinyl. It is also harder, so it resists denting from dropped objects and furniture legs better.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where Vinyl Wins</h3>

          <p>
            Water, decisively. Vinyl has no wood in the core, so standing water does nothing to it. That makes it the right answer for bathrooms, laundries, and basements regardless of what a laminate warranty claims. It is also softer and warmer underfoot, and quieter without special underlayment.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How to Choose Room by Room</h3>

          <p>
            Use laminate in living rooms, bedrooms, dining rooms, and hallways where realism and dent resistance matter and floods are unlikely. Use vinyl in bathrooms, laundries, basements, and any room over a slab with moisture history. Many houses are best served by using both.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cost Is Close Enough to Ignore</h3>

          <p>
            Quality laminate and quality luxury vinyl land in a similar price band, so the decision should be made on performance and appearance rather than budget. Very cheap examples of either category disappoint in the same ways.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 7: What a Laminate Floor Actually Costs -->
        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. What a Laminate Floor Actually Costs
          </h2>

          <p>
            The price per square foot on the box is roughly half the story. Budgeting the whole job prevents the mid-project discovery that turns a cheap floor into an expensive weekend.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>The flooring itself</strong> spans a wide band. Budget AC3 laminate sits at the bottom, mainstream AC4 in the middle, and thick AC5 or premium wide-plank collections at the top. The gap between AC3 and AC4 is usually small enough that stepping up is the easiest good decision in the whole project.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Add ten percent for waste.</strong> Cuts at the ends of rows, around obstacles, and for damaged boards mean you will not use every square foot you buy. Ten percent is the standard allowance and fifteen is safer for a room with lots of angles or if you are laying planks diagonally.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Underlayment and vapour barrier</strong> add a real line item unless the planks have a pad attached. Over concrete a barrier is mandatory rather than optional, and a decent acoustic pad is the cheapest upgrade available to how the finished floor sounds.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Transitions, trim, and thresholds</strong> are routinely forgotten and add up quickly across a whole house. Every doorway needs a transition strip, every wall needs the expansion gap covered, and matching pieces from the flooring manufacturer cost considerably more than generic ones.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Subfloor preparation is the wildcard.</strong> If a flatness check reveals dips beyond tolerance, levelling compound or plywood sheathing becomes part of the job. This is the item most likely to surprise you, which is why checking flatness before ordering anything is worth the twenty minutes.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Professional installation</strong> typically runs in the same range as the material itself, sometimes more for complex layouts. Laminate is genuinely DIY-friendly, so this is one of the larger savings available to anyone willing to spend a weekend on it.
          </p>
        </div>

