              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What AC rating do I need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                AC4 is the right default for most homes. It covers entryways, kitchens, and hallways as well as living areas, where AC3 is rated only for general residential use and shows a worn path in high-traffic spots. Step up to AC5 for an entry hall that takes constant outdoor grit or a home with large dogs.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is laminate waterproof?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not in the way vinyl is. Laminate has a fibreboard core that swells permanently if water reaches it, so waterproof claims refer to surface and joint treatments that keep spills out for a stated period, typically 24 to 72 hours. That covers ordinary accidents but not a burst pipe, a leaking appliance, or a damp slab.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does thickness matter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not much for durability, which is governed by the wear layer and AC rating, but a lot for how the floor sounds and feels. Thin 6 to 8mm planks produce the hollow click most people associate with cheap laminate, while 10 to 12mm feels solid and bridges minor subfloor dips that thin planks flex into.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I install it myself?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, it is among the most DIY-friendly floors available. The failures are rarely about skill: acclimate the boxes in the room for at least 48 hours, leave a 3/16 inch expansion gap at every wall and fixed object, check the subfloor is flat within about 3/16 inch over 10 feet, and never fasten a floating floor down anywhere.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Laminate or vinyl plank?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Laminate looks and feels more like real wood and resists denting better, so it suits living rooms, bedrooms, and hallways. Vinyl has no wood in its core and is genuinely unaffected by standing water, so it belongs in bathrooms, laundries, and basements. Pricing is close enough that the decision should be made on the room.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Laminate Flooring Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Laminate failures are almost never about the product being bad. They are about installing it in the wrong room or skipping a step that takes minutes.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying AC3 and Installing It Everywhere</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                AC3 is rated for general residential use, explicitly excluding entryways, kitchens, and busy hallways. Installed there it shows a visible worn path within a couple of years, which people then blame on laminate as a category. AC4 costs modestly more and covers the whole house.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Expansion Gap</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A floating floor expands and contracts with humidity and must be free to move. Butted tight to the walls it has nowhere to go and lifts into a ridge across the middle of the room, usually in the first humid season. Leave roughly 3/16 of an inch at every wall and fixed object and cover it with trim.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Laying Over Concrete Without a Vapour Barrier</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Slabs release moisture continuously, and laminate laid directly on one will cup and swell from beneath with no visible spill to explain it. Six mil polyethylene or an underlayment with a barrier built in prevents it entirely and costs very little.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Adding Underlayment Under Pre-Attached Pad</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Doubling the cushion feels like an upgrade and is the opposite. The floor becomes too soft, planks flex more than the locking joints tolerate, and seams separate or crack over time. Use the attached pad or a separate one, never both.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
